::ENG4_U04_L04_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which claim is strongest for an argument about later library hours during application season?
{
~A. Libraries are good and students are busy.#Incorrect. This is vague and not clearly arguable.
=B. Schools should offer limited later library hours during application season when supervision and safety procedures are planned.#Correct. This claim is specific, arguable, and evidence-based.
~C. Every school library should stay open all night forever.#Incorrect. This is extreme and unsupported.
~D. Application season exists.#Incorrect. This is a fact, not an argument claim.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Evidence: A counselor report says application completion increased during structured school work sessions. Which reasoning is strongest?
{
=A. The evidence supports structured sessions because it connects school resources with a measurable increase in application completion.#Correct. This explains why the evidence matters.
~B. The evidence proves students should never work at home.#Incorrect. This overstates the evidence.
~C. The evidence is about sports schedules.#Incorrect. This misreads the source.
~D. The evidence means every student completed every application.#Incorrect. This exaggerates the result.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which sentence uses "validity" correctly?
{
~A. The validity of the claim depends on how dramatic it sounds.#Incorrect. Validity depends on logic and support.
~B. Validity means the paragraph is long enough.#Incorrect. Length does not establish validity.
=C. The validity of the claim depends on whether the evidence logically supports it.#Correct. This accurately defines validity in argument.
~D. Validity means the writer likes the topic.#Incorrect. Personal preference is not validity.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Source set: Charging stations reduced short car trips but sidewalk blockage was a concern. Which claim uses both sources?
{
~A. Charging stations should be placed anywhere because they are convenient.#Incorrect. This ignores sidewalk access concerns.
~B. Charging stations should never be considered.#Incorrect. This ignores the transportation benefit.
~C. Charging stations are unrelated to transportation.#Incorrect. The source set directly discusses transportation.
=D. Local governments should fund charging stations when placement rules protect sidewalks and prioritize useful locations.#Correct. This claim balances benefit and implementation limits.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] What is the main job of reasoning in an argument paragraph?
{
~A. To repeat the claim in different words.#Incorrect. Repetition does not explain evidence.
=B. To explain how evidence supports, limits, or clarifies the claim.#Correct. Reasoning makes the evidence meaningful.
~C. To replace all evidence with opinion.#Incorrect. Reasoning works with evidence.
~D. To make the paragraph longer without adding meaning.#Incorrect. Reasoning must add logical explanation.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Which reasoning is invalid for the claim "Cities should require reusable water stations at large events"?
{
~B. Plastic bottles were a common waste item after events, so water stations could target a documented source of waste.#Incorrect. This is valid reasoning from evidence.
=A. Water stations should be required because everyone who disagrees hates the environment.#Correct. This attacks opponents instead of using evidence.
~C. A pilot program lowered waste when stations were placed well, so placement rules matter.#Incorrect. This is valid reasoning from the pilot evidence.
~D. Setup costs matter, so the policy should include practical implementation rules.#Incorrect. This is valid reasoning that addresses a concern.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which word best describes evidence that directly supports a claim?
{
~A. Decorative#Incorrect. Evidence is not merely decorative.
~B. Random#Incorrect. Random evidence does not support a claim.
=D. Relevant#Correct. Relevant evidence directly connects to the claim.
~C. Unrelated#Incorrect. Unrelated evidence does not support the argument.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which claim is too broad for a source set about one city pilot program?
{
~A. The pilot suggests placement affects use.#Incorrect. This stays within the evidence.
~B. The city should review placement before expanding stations.#Incorrect. This is appropriately limited.
~D. Stations near transit stops may be more useful than stations in low-traffic areas.#Incorrect. This matches the source.
=C. Every city worldwide must build charging stations immediately.#Correct. This overextends beyond the source set.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Which research note best supports a claim about the need for safe charging-station placement?
{
=B. A neighborhood association warned that unclear placement can block sidewalks.#Correct. This directly supports the need for placement rules.
~A. A weather report predicted rain next week.#Incorrect. This is unrelated to placement.
~C. A source described the color of scooters.#Incorrect. This is not relevant to safety or placement.
~D. A restaurant menu listed lunch specials.#Incorrect. This does not support the claim.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Which option best evaluates the reasoning "The policy will work because I personally like it"?
{
~B. Strong, because personal preference is the same as evidence.#Incorrect. Personal preference does not prove a policy will work.
=A. Weak, because it does not connect the claim to evidence.#Correct. Valid reasoning requires evidence-based explanation.
~C. Strong, because the sentence is short.#Incorrect. Sentence length does not determine reasoning quality.
~D. Weak, because it uses academic vocabulary.#Incorrect. Vocabulary is not the problem; lack of evidence is.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which sentence best connects evidence to a claim?
{
~A. The report exists, so my claim is true.#Incorrect. This does not explain the evidence.
~B. Evidence is evidence and claims are claims.#Incorrect. This is circular and unhelpful.
=D. Because the report shows increased application completion, structured library sessions could help seniors finish important postsecondary tasks.#Correct. This clearly explains how evidence supports the claim.
~C. I have evidence somewhere, probably.#Incorrect. This is vague and unsupported.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which use of "sufficient" is correct?
{
~A. The evidence is sufficient because it is unrelated but interesting.#Incorrect. Sufficient evidence must support the claim.
~B. The claim is sufficient because it has no evidence.#Incorrect. A claim needs support.
~D. Sufficient means emotional.#Incorrect. Sufficiency concerns amount and quality of support.
=C. The evidence is sufficient when it provides enough relevant support for the claim.#Correct. This accurately explains sufficiency.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Which source would best help evaluate whether later library hours are practical?
{
=B. A staffing memo explaining supervision needs and safety procedures.#Correct. Practicality depends on implementation details.
~A. A poem about silence.#Incorrect. This does not evaluate implementation.
~C. A list of unrelated school mascots.#Incorrect. This source is irrelevant.
~D. A restaurant review.#Incorrect. This does not address school library hours.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Which reasoning correctly addresses a counterconcern?
{
~B. Setup costs do not exist because I dislike them.#Incorrect. This dismisses rather than addresses the concern.
=A. Although setup requires staff and space, placement rules and permit requirements can make water stations practical.#Correct. This acknowledges the concern and explains a response.
~C. Anyone who mentions cost is wrong.#Incorrect. This attacks people instead of reasoning.
~D. Costs are boring, so they should not be discussed.#Incorrect. This avoids a relevant concern.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which claim is most arguable?
{
~A. Electric bikes exist.#Incorrect. This is a fact, not a claim.
~B. Some people use transportation.#Incorrect. This is too general.
=D. Public charging stations should be funded near transit stops when placement rules protect sidewalk access.#Correct. This is specific and arguable.
~C. Sidewalks are outside.#Incorrect. This is not an argument.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which phrase best shows precision in argument writing?
{
~A. good stuff#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~B. things that help#Incorrect. This lacks specific meaning.
~D. a lot of people maybe#Incorrect. This is imprecise.
=C. structured application support for seniors#Correct. This phrase is specific and academically precise.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Why should a claim include a condition when sources show both benefits and implementation concerns?
{
=B. The condition keeps the claim aligned with the full evidence set.#Correct. A condition helps the claim reflect benefits and limits.
~A. The condition makes the claim impossible to understand.#Incorrect. A clear condition increases precision.
~C. The condition removes the need for evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is still needed.
~D. The condition hides the main argument.#Incorrect. A good condition clarifies the argument.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Which statement best describes valid reasoning?
{
~B. Reasoning is valid when it is emotional enough.#Incorrect. Emotion does not establish validity.
=A. Reasoning is valid when the explanation logically connects evidence to the claim.#Correct. This is the central standard for valid reasoning.
~C. Reasoning is valid when it ignores evidence.#Incorrect. Valid reasoning uses evidence.
~D. Reasoning is valid when it is unrelated to the issue.#Incorrect. Relevance matters.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Evidence: Mock interviews led students to revise resumes and prepare examples. Which claim does this best support?
{
~A. Students should never write resumes.#Incorrect. This contradicts the evidence.
~B. Interviews are unrelated to career preparation.#Incorrect. The evidence is directly related.
=D. Career-readiness workshops can strengthen preparation by giving students structured practice.#Correct. This claim follows logically from the evidence.
~C. Every student will get hired after one workshop.#Incorrect. This overstates the evidence.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which sentence uses "implementation" correctly?
{
~A. Implementation means the argument has no plan.#Incorrect. Implementation refers to carrying out a plan.
=C. The policy's implementation should include staffing, placement, and safety procedures.#Correct. This uses implementation to describe how the policy would be carried out.
~B. Implementation is the emotional tone of the source.#Incorrect. Tone is different from implementation.
~D. Implementation means copying a source exactly.#Incorrect. This is unrelated to the word.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Which evidence is least relevant to a claim about reusable water stations at public events?
{
=B. A historical note about the first city park sign.#Correct. This does not connect to reusable water stations, waste, cost, or placement.
~A. A sanitation report about plastic bottle waste after events.#Incorrect. This is relevant evidence.
~C. An organizer's note about rental fees and volunteers.#Incorrect. This is relevant to implementation.
~D. A pilot report about station placement and waste reduction.#Incorrect. This is directly relevant.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Which reasoning best explains Source B: "Reusable water stations require rental fees, setup space, and volunteers"?
{
~B. The policy should ignore costs because costs are inconvenient.#Incorrect. This avoids the evidence.
=A. The policy should include implementation requirements because the source shows the solution needs planning to work.#Correct. This uses the source to refine the argument.
~C. The policy is impossible in every situation.#Incorrect. The source identifies challenges, not impossibility.
~D. The source is about weather.#Incorrect. This misreads the source.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which option is evidence rather than reasoning?
{
~A. This matters because structured practice builds readiness.#Incorrect. This is reasoning.
~B. Therefore, the policy should include supervision.#Incorrect. This is reasoning or claim development.
=D. A senior survey found that many students lacked quiet work time after school.#Correct. This is evidence from a source.
~C. The evidence supports the claim because it shows need.#Incorrect. This is reasoning about evidence.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which academic word best completes the sentence: "The evidence is _____ because it directly addresses the claim."
{
~A. decorative#Incorrect. Decorative does not describe useful evidence.
~B. accidental#Incorrect. Accidental does not show connection to the claim.
~D. unrelated#Incorrect. Unrelated means the opposite of the intended meaning.
=C. relevant#Correct. Relevant evidence directly addresses the claim.
}
::ENG4_U04_L04_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which revision improves the claim "Schools should help seniors"?
{
=B. Schools should provide structured application work sessions for seniors during application season when staffing and safety procedures are available.#Correct. This revision adds audience, action, timing, and condition.
~A. Schools should help seniors because helping is good.#Incorrect. This remains vague and circular.
~C. Seniors exist and schools exist.#Incorrect. This is not an argument.
~D. Schools should do all helpful things all the time.#Incorrect. This is too broad and unrealistic.
}
